    /// <summary>A ring that shows that something is in progress (WinUI's `ProgressRing`): a spinning arc while [`is_indeterminate`](Self::is_indeterminate) (the default), the part of a ring of the [`value`](Self::value) between [`minimum`](Self::minimum) and [`maximum`](Self::maximum) otherwise, and nothing while not [`is_active`](Self::is_active). As in WinUI, `Value`, `Minimum` and `Maximum` are properties of the ring (not of `RangeBase`), coerced as `ProgressRing.cpp` coerces them: the bound set last wins, and a value outside the range is moved into it. The control sets the states of WinUI as pseudo-classes, which the control theme of the WinUI theme (`src/FerroUI.Themes.WinUI/Controls/ProgressRing.xaml`) styles: `:active` (Active), `:determinate-active` (DeterminateActive) and `:inactive` (Inactive). WinUI draws the ring with an `AnimatedVisualPlayer` and the Lottie compositions of its animated visuals, which the code plays. FerroUI has no player: the template draws the ring with `Arc` shapes, and the control moves the arc of the template part `PART_Indicator` as the code of WinUI plays the compositions: the indeterminate composition loops over two seconds (the arc grows to half the ring and shrinks while it turns 900 degrees), and a determinate change plays the determinate composition from the old to the new value (two seconds for the whole ring), a decrease at once. The clock is the ring's `Clock`, or the global clock; the motion stops while the ring is inactive, hidden or out of the visual tree. `DeterminateSource` and `IndeterminateSource` (preview members of WinUI) are not ported. The Fluent and Simple themes have no template for it (docs/porting/winui-controls.md, section 5.1).</summary>
